% ── Title ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{center}
    {\LARGE\bfseries A Terrain-Thermal Convergence Framework for Agricultural Climate Suitability: Evidence from Hawaiian Specialty Coffee}

    \vspace{12pt}

    {\large Simon-Hans Edasi}\\[4pt]
    Independent Researcher, GeoGastronomy Project\\[8pt]

    \textbf{Corresponding author:} simonhansedasi@gmail.com\\[4pt]

    \textbf{Keywords:} specialty agriculture; climate suitability; terrain feasibility; CMIP6 projections; terroir; feasibility set; Hawaiian coffee

    \vspace{6pt}
    \hrule
\end{center}

\vspace{8pt}

% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{center}
\textbf{Abstract}
\end{center}

A thermal optimum migrates on decadal timescales; the terrain configuration that gives a terroir-bound crop its identity is fixed on geological ones. Climate-only suitability projections implicitly offer adaptation by relocation, but where a product's value derives from particular ground, the optimum can move and the crop cannot follow. We formalise this as the intersection of a moving thermal set with a stationary terrain set---a terrain-thermal convergence framework combining a Gaussian thermal niche with a district-scale, identity-anchored topographic feasibility screen---and apply it to Hawaiian specialty coffee's two producing districts, Kona and Ka\okina u, on a 500\,m island-wide grid ($n = 317$ labelled farm cells). The two districts' thermal optima are statistically indistinguishable (difference $0.12$\,\textdegree C, 95\% CI $-0.08$ to $+0.32$\,\textdegree C)---a climate-only projection cannot separate them---yet their terrain footprints are small and fixed (3.8\% of the Big Island matches Kona's agro-ecological profile and 4.8\% matches Ka\okina u's under the primary screen; 4.6\% and 3.0\% under a decorrelated distance metric): the two districts diverge through terrain, not thermal sensitivity. Evaluated across the full farmable island surface, warming appears to increase thermal suitability for roughly 70\% of farmable land under either district's envelope---a figure we show is equivalent, to within one percent, to the statement that 70\% of farmable land lies above $\approx$600\,m, and therefore a description of the island's hypsometry rather than an independent climatic result. Restricted to each district's own terrain the sign reverses: 73\% of Kona's and 80\% of Ka\okina u's labelled farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, rising to 83\% and 88\% by 2045. The mechanism is visible in the horizon-to-horizon change: between 2035 and 2045 the island-wide thermally suitable area is static to within one percent and the terrain footprint is unchanged by construction, yet their intersection contracts by 16\% and 21\%---neither set shrinks, but the moving one slides off the fixed one. Terrain-constrained suitable area is roughly an order of magnitude smaller than the thermal-only estimate, but comparison against a size-matched null shows that this multiplier reflects the size and placement of the terrain footprint rather than any property of the districts---random patches of equal area yield larger factors 97--100\% of the time---so we report it as a sign, not a magnitude. For a terroir-bound crop the upslope migration that climate-only projections imply is therefore largely unavailable, and the operative question under warming is not how much land becomes climatically suitable but how long the suitable band and the identity-bearing terrain remain in register.

\vspace{10pt}
\hrule
\vspace{10pt}

% ── 1. Introduction ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The state of Hawai\okina i produces the great majority of commercially grown specialty coffee in the United States (smaller commercial production also exists in Puerto Rico and California); ``100\% Kona'' certification commands substantial wholesale price premiums, making geographic identity the foundation of economic value. That specificity is also the product's fundamental climate vulnerability: unlike commodity crops, adaptation cannot simply mean planting more acres elsewhere.

Regional climate projections indicate warming of approximately $+1.0$\,\textdegree C by the 2035 epoch and $+1.35$\,\textdegree C by 2045 across the Big Island, from ensemble CMIP6 projections \citep{Thrasher2022}. For illustration, applying the literature-reported environmental lapse rate of $-6.5$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ for the islands (below the $\approx$2{,}150\,m trade-wind inversion that structures Big Island climate; \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020, GiambellucaNullet1991})---not the real observed climatology this paper's own analysis uses (\S\ref{sec:methods})---this warming translates to an upslope migration of the thermal optimum for \textit{Coffea arabica} of roughly 154\,m by 2035 and 208\,m by 2045: a rough scale-setting figure, not a result of the analysis below, but still a shift that represents a substantial fraction of the entire Kona production belt within the planning horizon of farms being established today.

Prior assessments of coffee suitability under climate change have largely treated temperature as the primary screen and land as uniformly available within the resulting thermal window \citep{Bunn2015, Laderach2011, Davis2012, OvalleRivera2015}. While these studies often incorporate precipitation, soil, and vegetation indices, these variables are typically evaluated across the full geographic surface rather than as a constraint on feasible cultivation space \citep{Hijmans2005, Elith2009}. Species distribution models such as MaxEnt \citep{Phillips2006} extend this approach by modelling the realized niche from occurrence records, but the fundamental output remains a probability surface over geographic space---a surface that treats all land as broadly available for occupation given the right environmental conditions. Mechanistic niche models address some of these limitations by incorporating explicit physiological and spatial constraints \citep{Kearney2009}, but even these are rarely applied with terrain feasibility screens in agricultural contexts. For wild species, treating climatic space as the primary constraint is a reasonable assumption. For specialty agriculture, it is not: a farm requires not merely suitable climate but accessible, cultivable, legally available, and economically viable land \citep{Foley2011}. These approaches rarely distinguish between climatically suitable land and land that is physically or economically cultivable; in volcanic, orographic specialty crop systems this assumption fails systematically, as thermal projections evaluated across the full island surface include steep lava fields, isolated ridgelines, and hydrologically disconnected terrain that satisfy a temperature criterion but cannot support agriculture.

The argument of this paper is a mismatch of timescales. A thermal optimum moves on decadal timescales; the terrain configuration that defines a terroir-bound crop's identity---elevation band, aspect, slope, parent material---is fixed on geological ones. Where a crop's value derives from that configuration, the optimum can migrate but the crop cannot follow it, because the land it would migrate onto is not the land that makes the product what it is. Adaptation-by-relocation, which climate-only projections implicitly offer, is therefore largely unavailable to such systems, and the constraint binds hardest exactly where geographic identity carries the most economic weight.

Formally, let $\mathcal{T}_r \subseteq \mathcal{G}$ denote the set of grid cells whose terrain falls within the observed agro-ecological envelope of region $r$ (the \textit{topographic feasibility set}), and let $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ denote the set of cells where the thermal suitability score $S_r$ exceeds a given level under warming $\Delta T$ (the \textit{thermal suitability set}). Climate-only assessments implicitly treat $\mathcal{C}_r$ as the adaptation opportunity. The terrain-thermal convergence framework replaces this with the intersection $\mathcal{F}_r(\Delta T) = \mathcal{T}_r \cap \mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$---the \textit{feasible set}---the only geographic space where both conditions are simultaneously satisfied. The decisive property is that $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ moves with warming while $\mathcal{T}_r$ does not: the thermal set migrates upslope over decades, the terrain set is a fact about the volcano. The quantity of interest is therefore not the size of any one intersection but its behaviour as $\Delta T$ grows---specifically, whether $\mathcal{F}_r$ can track the optimum or is progressively squeezed against a boundary it cannot cross. In plain terms: $\mathcal{C}_r$ is the thermal ceiling---all land that is warm enough; $\mathcal{T}_r$ is the terrain floor---all land that is actually coffee-country; $\mathcal{F}_r$ is their intersection---the only space where both conditions hold simultaneously. Climate-only projections report the ceiling; this framework reports the floor. This parallels the Hutchinsonian distinction between fundamental niche (all physiologically tolerable space) and realized niche (the subset actually occupied given additional constraints) \citep{Hutchinson1957}: $\mathcal{C}_r$ is the thermal fundamental niche; $\mathcal{F}_r$ is the terrain-constrained realized agricultural space.

Masking a suitability surface by land availability is not itself new: global assessments such as GAEZ and the crop-suitability literature routinely exclude protected, urban, or non-arable land, and Ovalle-Rivera et al.\ \citep{OvalleRivera2015} explicitly caveat that upslope Colombian terrain may not be convertible. What that literature does not supply, and what a terroir-bound crop requires, is a screen anchored to the \textit{specific} agro-ecological configuration a designation of origin refers to, at the scale a district actually occupies. A generic arability mask asks whether land can grow coffee; the screen used here asks whether land is Kona, which is the question the price premium is denominated in. That is the contribution: a district-scale, identity-anchored feasibility screen, and the demonstration that under such a screen the adaptation space implied by thermal projection alone is largely inaccessible.

Applied to the Big Island, this framework asks not ``where is the temperature right?'' but ``where is the temperature right \textit{and} the terrain actually coffee-country?'' The answer is spatially compact, and---contrary to the expectation that two districts on opposite flanks of the same volcano face opposite fates---closely similar for both producing districts, which turn out to be thermally indistinguishable and to differ only in where their fixed terrain happens to sit.

% ── 2. Study Area ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Study Area}
\label{sec:studyarea}

The Island of Hawai\okina i is a shield volcano rising to 4{,}205\,m at Mauna Kea. Its geometry creates dramatic climatic gradients over short distances: the windward northeast receives over 5{,}000\,mm of annual rainfall at mid-elevations, while the leeward west and south flanks are substantially drier. Over 94\% of the island's land surface has slopes below 25\textdegree, reflecting the low-gradient basaltic shield; slope alone rarely bars cultivation in the way it would in a faulted or dissected mountain landscape---the constraint that binds here is agro-ecological terrain-matching (elevation band, aspect, parent material), not access to farmable gradient.

Two coffee-producing districts occupy distinct niches on this terrain (Figure~\ref{fig:studyarea}). \textbf{Kona} farms (592 polygons, $\approx$4{,}800\,acres) cluster on the leeward west slope of Mauna Loa at 200--950\,m (farm-cell mean 539.2\,m, $\sigma=145.6$\,m, $n=258$), where afternoon orographic clouds moderate peak temperatures and annual rainfall averages 1{,}200--2{,}000\,mm. Its topographic profile is tightly constrained: a compact north-south corridor of west-facing slopes with moderate relief and low coastal distance. \textbf{Ka\okina u} farms (82 polygons, $\approx$1{,}200\,acres) occupy a south-facing terrace at 250--750\,m (farm-cell mean 515.9\,m, $\sigma=111.1$\,m, $n=59$) with a drier, more variable climate (700--900\,mm) and deep Andisols derived from Mauna Loa basalt. Ka\okina u's broader elevation range and greater aspect diversity produce a topographic signature distinct enough that the two districts occupy well-separated regions of standardised terrain-feature space (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}).

The two districts' thermal niches, by contrast, overlap almost completely once evaluated on a common temperature surface. Fitting separate Gaussian envelopes to each district's farm cells from real observed temperature (Kodama et al.\ 2024 gridded climatology, \S\ref{sec:methods}) yields nearly coincident optima---$\mu_{\text{Kona}} = 20.75$\,\textdegree C ($\sigma_{\text{Kona}} = 1.27$\,\textdegree C, $n=258$) versus $\mu_{\text{Ka'u}} = 20.87$\,\textdegree C ($\sigma_{\text{Ka'u}} = 1.01$\,\textdegree C, $n=59$), with Kona the wider tolerance band of the two. The $0.12$\,\textdegree C separation is not statistically distinguishable from zero (Welch $t = 1.21$, $p = 0.23$; 95\% CI $-0.08$ to $+0.32$\,\textdegree C), and because each district's farm cells are spatially contiguous their effective sample size is smaller than $n$, which widens the interval further. The appropriate reading is not that the optima differ by a small amount but that they cannot be told apart at all on this evidence. The districts do not diverge thermally; they diverge only once each envelope is intersected with its own terrain footprint, which is why a district-level, dual-screen analysis is still necessary (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}).

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.60\textwidth]{Fig1.jpg}
\caption{\textbf{Study area.} Coffee farm polygons (orange) from the 2020 Hawai\okina i Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021}. The Kona belt occupies the leeward west flank of Mauna Loa (upper cluster, 592 polygons); Ka\okina u farms sit on the south-facing terrace (lower cluster, 82 polygons). The 674 polygons totalling $\approx$6{,}000\,acres are the full mapped commercial coffee-farm footprint on the Big Island in this dataset---not a claim about the U.S.\ specialty coffee industry as a whole, which includes substantial acreage (e.g.\ Kaua\okina i Coffee, $\approx$3{,}100\,acres) outside it.}
\label{fig:studyarea}
\end{figure}

% ── 3. Results ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Results}

\subsection{The Terrain Footprint Is Small and Does Not Move}

The island-wide topographic identity map (Figure~\ref{fig:identity}) reveals the pre-existing structure of geographic constraint. Kona-like terrain (blue) forms a compact linear corridor on the west leeward flank at 150--950\,m; Ka\okina u-like terrain (red) concentrates on the south-facing terrace (185--895\,m) with limited extension onto adjacent elevated terrain. The combined topographic footprint covers only 8.7\% of island land (Kona 3.8\%, Ka\okina u 4.8\%); the remaining 91.3\% lacks the agro-ecological signature of either district. Because assignment is by nearer centroid subject to a distance cutoff, the two footprints are non-overlapping by construction and their sum is arithmetic, not an empirical finding.

The property that matters for what follows is not the size of this footprint but its fixity. Every feature defining it---mean elevation, elevation deviation, maximum slope, aspect, total and local relief, coastal distance---is a property of the volcano's constructional history and subsequent erosion, and none of them respond to a decadal warming signal. Mauna Loa's west flank will present the same slope, aspect and elevation band in 2045 that it presents now; what changes is only which part of it is at the right temperature. Figure~\ref{fig:identity} is therefore not a snapshot of a moving quantity but the fixed domain into which every subsequent thermal projection must be read, and the asymmetry between a terrain set that cannot move and a thermal set that must is the whole of the argument. Everything reported below is a consequence of intersecting a moving set with a stationary one.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{Fig2.jpg}
\caption{\textbf{Topographic identity map.} Each 500\,m island land cell scored by Euclidean distance in standardised 8-feature terrain space to the Kona (blue) and Ka\okina u (red) farm centroids. Cells outside the 95th-percentile centroid-distance threshold for both regions are shown in grey. Only 3.8\% of island land is Kona-like and 4.8\% is Ka\okina u-like---the terrain domain into which all thermal projections must be read. Sensitivity across threshold choices is reported in Supplementary Fig.~S1.}
\label{fig:identity}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The Thermal Optimum Migrates Upslope}
\label{sec:results:thermal}

Figure~\ref{fig:thermal} shows thermal suitability change across the full farmable island surface, evaluated by regional envelope without applying the terrain screen. Under the Kona envelope, 69.2\% of farmable cells gain thermal suitability by 2035 (67.9\% by 2045); under the Ka\okina u envelope, 70.2\% gain by 2035 (68.8\% by 2045). Because the two envelopes' optima are statistically indistinguishable (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}), the climate-only trajectories are nearly indistinguishable at the island-wide scale shown here---the districts diverge only once each is restricted to its own terrain footprint (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}).

These island-wide fractions should not be over-read, and we state their content plainly. Since $\Delta S_r > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$ (\S\ref{sec:methods}) and the temperature field is $98\%$ explained by elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods}), each fraction is equivalent to a single elevation contour: gain under the Kona envelope at 2035 means $T < 20.25$\,\textdegree C, i.e.\ $z \gtrsim 607$\,m, and the two classifications agree on 99.1\% of farmable cells (Ka\okina u 2035: $z \gtrsim 583$\,m, 99.0\% agreement; both districts at 2045: $z \gtrsim 642$\,m and $617$\,m, $\geq$99.2\%). ``Roughly 70\% of farmable land gains'' is therefore, to within one percent, the statement ``roughly 70\% of farmable Big Island land lies above $\approx$600\,m''---a fact about the hypsometry of a shield volcano, not an independent climatic finding. It is reported here because it is the quantity a climate-only assessment would produce, and the contrast with the terrain-restricted result (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}) is the paper's subject; it is not itself evidence of anything.

