\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig3A.jpg}
    \caption{Kona thermal envelope ($\mu = 20.75$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma = 1.27$\,\textdegree C). 69.2\% of farmable cells gain thermal suitability by $\sim$2035, easing to 67.9\% by $\sim$2045.}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig3B.jpg}
    \caption{Ka\okina u thermal envelope ($\mu = 20.87$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma = 1.01$\,\textdegree C). 70.2\% of farmable cells gain by $\sim$2035, easing to 68.8\% by $\sim$2045. Topographic screen not applied.}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{\textbf{Thermal suitability change by $\sim$2035 across the full farmable island surface ($\Delta T \approx +1.00$\,\textdegree C; ensemble mean ACCESS-CM2 $\times$ MPI-ESM1-2-HR, SSP2-4.5 $\times$ SSP5-8.5).} Red = increased thermal alignment; blue = reduced alignment. Grey = slope $>25$\textdegree. These maps represent the climate-only, direction-of-change view: for both districts alike, restricting to actual terrain and a genuine absolute suitability threshold reduces accessible thermal opportunity 7- to 11-fold (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}). Compare with Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}.}
\label{fig:thermal}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What Remains: The Feasible Set Under Warming}
\label{sec:results:convergence}

Applying the topographic screen collapses the apparent thermal opportunity into a much smaller feasible domain, and reveals a pattern common to both districts rather than one unique to either (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}).

Within Kona-like terrain, screened cells are predominantly blue at the lower, coastal-facing portion of the belt---where most existing farms sit (Figure~\ref{fig:thermal}a farm markers)---with a red, gaining fringe concentrated at the upper elevation margin. Overall, 66.1\% of Kona-like farmable terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (70.8\% by 2045); the remaining 33.9\%/29.2\% gains, concentrated in that upper fringe.

Within Ka\okina u-like terrain, the same pattern recurs: a lower, red-to-blue transition near the coast where warming has already pushed conditions past the optimum, and gains concentrated toward the upper margin. Overall, 67.5\% of Ka\okina u-like farmable terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (73.5\% by 2045); the remaining 32.5\%/26.5\% gains.

The central result of this study is what happens to that pattern when it is evaluated on land that is actually farmed rather than land that merely matches the district's terrain profile. Existing farms are not distributed uniformly across their district's footprint; they sit preferentially in its lower, warmer portion, which is where the optimum is departing from first. Evaluated on the 258 labelled Kona farm cells, \textbf{73.3\%} are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, rising to \textbf{83.3\%} by 2045; for Ka\okina u's 59 farm cells the figures are \textbf{79.7\%} and \textbf{88.1\%}. In acreage terms this is roughly 3{,}516 of Kona's $\approx$4{,}800 farmed acres and 956 of Ka\okina u's $\approx$1{,}200 by 2035, rising to approximately 4{,}000 and 1{,}058 by 2045. These are larger than the terrain-level fractions above precisely because the terrain footprint includes upper-elevation ground that farms do not currently occupy.

That upper ground is the only direction the optimum is moving, and it is where the argument of the paper bites. The gaining fringe exists, but it lies at the upper margin of a footprint that does not extend further upslope: beyond it, terrain stops resembling Kona or Ka\okina u---the slope steepens onto the volcano's upper constructional flanks, aspect and relief depart from the observed farm envelope, and substrate ages fall away. Warming therefore does not open new coffee country; it relocates the favourable band within a fixed one, and the band is running out of footprint to move into. The clearest expression of this is what happens between the two horizons. Island-wide, the thermally suitable area is essentially unchanged from 2035 to 2045---it falls 0.4\% under the Kona envelope and \textit{rises} 0.7\% under Ka\okina u's---and the terrain set, by construction, does not change at all. Yet the feasible set, their intersection, contracts by 16.4\% (Kona, 973 to 813 cells) and 20.6\% (Ka\okina u, 957 to 760). Neither input shrinks; the intersection does, because the moving set is sliding off the stationary one. No amount of additional warming-driven thermal opportunity elsewhere on the island can compensate, because that opportunity is not on terrain that is Kona or Ka\okina u.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth, height=0.82\textheight, keepaspectratio]{Fig4.jpg}
\caption{\textbf{Terrain-thermal convergence map---the decision surface of this study.} Top: $\sim$2035 ($\Delta T \approx +1.00$\,\textdegree C); bottom: $\sim$2045 ($\Delta T \approx +1.35$\,\textdegree C). Unlike conventional thermal suitability maps, this figure visualises only the subset of land where climatic change intersects with geomorphologically feasible agricultural terrain. Only cells passing the 95th-percentile topographic screen are coloured, each receiving the thermal delta matched to its regional envelope (Kona-like terrain $\to$ Kona $\Delta S$; Ka\okina u-like terrain $\to$ Ka\okina u $\Delta S$). Mid-grey: farmable terrain outside both profiles---land where the thermal signal may be favourable but the terrain constraint is binding. Light grey: non-farmable (slope $> 25$\textdegree). The coloured footprint is the constrained feasible set: the only geographic space where thermal adaptation signals are simultaneously grounded in terrain reality. In both districts, warming pushes the thermally-favourable zone upslope within a terrain envelope that does not itself expand: the lower, currently-farmed portion of each belt trends blue (declining), while a fringe at the upper elevation margin trends red (gaining). Because the topographic screen's own feature set includes mean elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}), this upper-fringe gain is consistent with, but does not prove, a genuine agro-ecological upslope refuge---the screen cannot by construction rule out that a cell moving far enough upslope to gain thermal suitability is partly an artefact of also moving away from each district's current elevation profile (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}). Climate signals outside the coloured footprint lack terrain grounding and cannot be interpreted as actionable adaptation opportunity for coffee production.}
\label{fig:convergence}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Why We Do Not Report an Overestimation Multiplier as the Result}
\label{sec:results:overestimation}

A natural way to summarise the preceding sections would be a single ratio: thermally suitable area divided by terrain-constrained suitable area, reported as the factor by which a climate-only assessment overstates accessible adaptation space. Earlier versions of this analysis did exactly that. We report the ratio below for completeness and because it is the quantity a reader will otherwise compute, but we argue in this section that it should not be treated as the study's result, and we present the evidence that persuaded us of this. The finding of the paper is the structural one in \S\ref{sec:results:convergence}---a moving thermal set sliding off a stationary terrain set---not a multiplier. We define:
\[
    \text{Overestimation factor} =
    \frac{|\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)|}{|\mathcal{F}_r(\Delta T)|}, \qquad
    \mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T) = \{g \in \mathcal{G} : S_r(T_g, \Delta T) > \tau\}, \quad \tau = 0.5
\]
using the level-set thermal suitability set $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ (\S\ref{sec:methods}), not the direction-of-change statistic $\Delta S_r > 0$ reported in \S\ref{sec:results:convergence}'s district-specific trajectories below.

This ratio decomposes into two multiplicative terms: the ratio of thermal-only to terrain-constrained \textit{suitability rates} (the fraction of all farmable island cells exceeding $\tau$, divided by the fraction within the district's own terrain footprint exceeding $\tau$), times the inverse of the terrain fraction itself---an input taken directly from the farm-polygon data, not a finding of the thermal analysis. At the 95th-percentile baseline for 2035: for Kona, $9.0 = 0.393 \times 22.8$; for Ka\okina u, $7.2 = 0.350 \times 20.7$, where 22.8 and 20.7 are each district's $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$ (the inverse of its terrain fraction among farmable cells). Unlike a naive terrain-fraction bound, the terrain-thermal interaction term here (0.393, 0.350) is \textit{less than one}: each district's own terrain is already substantially more likely to be absolutely suitable than farmable land generally (61.4\% of Kona-like terrain exceeds $\tau$ versus 24.2\% of all farmable land; 54.9\% of Ka\okina u-like terrain versus 19.2\% island-wide)---because farms were sited, in the first place, on terrain that already sits closer to the district's own thermal optimum. The realized factor is therefore \textit{smaller} than the terrain-fraction-alone bound would predict, not larger, and the headline multiplier should be read as evidence for the terrain-thermal convergence framework's central claim---that suitability is a restricted intersection, not a continuous surface---rather than as a free-standing estimate of misdirected adaptation investment.

\textit{Ka\okina u.} Under the Ka\okina u envelope at $\sim$2035, 6{,}923 of the 36{,}055 farmable island cells (19.2\%) exceed the $\tau=0.5$ suitability threshold under future warming. Restricted to Ka\okina u-like terrain, 957 cells (2.7\% of farmable terrain) exceed it: overestimation factor = \textbf{7.2} at the 95th-percentile baseline. Across $\tau=0.1$--$0.8$, the factor ranges 6.3$\times$--8.0$\times$; across the terrain-threshold sensitivity range (90th--97.5th percentile) it ranges 5.5$\times$--13.7$\times$ under Euclidean distance and 9.7$\times$--60.7$\times$ under Mahalanobis distance (Supplementary Table~S1)---an order-of-magnitude overestimation does not hold at every threshold tested. By the 2045 horizon, thermal-only suitable area is 6{,}973 cells (19.3\%) and terrain-constrained suitable area is 760 cells (2.1\%), overestimation factor \textbf{9.2}. Restricted to Ka\okina u-like terrain specifically (1{,}743 farmable cells), 67.5\% is on a \textit{declining} thermal trajectory by 2035 (73.5\% by 2045). Evaluated instead on the district's 59 \textit{labelled farm cells}---the quantity relevant to existing growers---the decline fraction is higher still: 79.7\% by 2035 and 88.1\% by 2045, or roughly 956 to 1{,}058 of the district's $\approx$1{,}200 farmed acres. Farm cells decline faster than the terrain footprint as a whole because farms sit preferentially in the lower, warmer portion of the belt (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}); applying a terrain-level fraction to farmed acreage would understate the exposure of existing production. This direction-of-change statistic is unaffected by the $\tau$ threshold (\S\ref{sec:methods}).

\textit{Kona.} 8{,}711 of the 36{,}055 farmable island cells (24.2\%) exceed $\tau=0.5$ under the Kona envelope at $\sim$2035---a higher rate than Ka\okina u's, since Kona's slightly lower thermal optimum (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}) is closer to typical low-elevation Big Island temperatures. Restricted to Kona-like terrain, 973 cells (2.7\% of farmable terrain) exceed it: overestimation factor = \textbf{9.0} at the 95th-percentile baseline, rising to \textbf{10.7} by 2045 (8{,}680 cells thermal-only, 813 terrain-constrained). Across $\tau=0.1$--$0.8$, the factor ranges 7.9$\times$--10.3$\times$, and across the terrain-threshold range (90th--97.5th percentile) 8.0$\times$--11.5$\times$. Restricted to Kona-like terrain specifically (1{,}584 farmable cells), 66.1\% is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035 (70.8\% by 2045); on the district's 258 labelled farm cells the figure is again higher---73.3\% by 2035 and 83.3\% by 2045, roughly 3{,}516 to 4{,}000 of the district's $\approx$4{,}800 farmed acres. The gaining minority concentrates at the upper elevation margin of the belt. Because the topographic screen's own feature set includes mean elevation (\S\ref{sec:methods:topo}), a cell that moves far enough upslope to gain thermal suitability necessarily also moves away from the Kona centroid in elevation space; whether that upper-fringe gain reflects a genuine agro-ecological refuge or is partly an artefact of including elevation directly in the screen is a question this analysis does not resolve (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}).

The overestimation is a geometric consequence of evaluating suitability over a continuous surface when the realizable niche occupies a restricted terrain subset, but its magnitude is not a stable property of either district, and we recommend against reading it as one. It is not invariant to threshold or distance-metric choice, spanning 5.5$\times$--60.7$\times$ for Ka\okina u across defensible alternatives (Supplementary Table~S1); it is mildly sensitive to the $1.5\times$ envelope-width inflation (\S\ref{sec:methods}), moving from 8.1$\times$ to 9.5$\times$ (Kona) and 6.4$\times$ to 7.6$\times$ (Ka\okina u) as the inflation runs from $1.0\times$ to $2.0\times$; and, most importantly, it is not distinctive of the districts at all.

To test the last point we constructed a null distribution, drawing 200 random size-matched compact patches of farmable land and computing the same factor for each. The observed factors sit at the \textit{low} end of that null: Kona's 9.0$\times$ against a null median of 18.0$\times$ (97\% of random patches yield a larger factor), Ka\okina u's 7.2$\times$ against a null median of 15.3$\times$ (100\% yield a larger factor). A randomly placed patch of the same size is typically \textit{more} ``overestimated'' than the real coffee districts. This is the same fact the decomposition above states in different terms---each district's terrain is disproportionately likely to be thermally suitable already, because farms were sited there---but it makes the consequence explicit: the overestimation factor measures patch size and placement, not an agro-ecological property of Kona or Ka\okina u, and it cannot be used to argue that these districts are unusually exposed. What survives is the qualitative structure, not the multiplier: terrain-constrained suitable area is several-fold smaller than the thermal-only estimate under every setting tested, for both districts, which is the sign of the effect and the reason a feasibility screen is necessary at all.

% ── 4. Discussion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Discussion}

A thermal optimum that migrates upslope is only an adaptation opportunity if there is upslope land the crop can actually occupy. For a terroir-bound crop there generally is not, because the terrain configuration that defines the product is fixed on a timescale orders of magnitude longer than the one on which the optimum moves. That mismatch is the paper's argument, and Hawaiian coffee makes it unusually legible: between 2035 and 2045 the island-wide thermally suitable area is flat to within one percent and the terrain footprint is unchanged by construction, yet the intersection of the two---the land that is simultaneously warm enough and actually Kona or Ka\okina u---contracts by 16--21\% (\S\ref{sec:results:convergence}). Nothing shrank except the alignment between a set that moves and a set that cannot.

The consequence for existing production is direct: 73\% of Kona's and 80\% of Ka\okina u's labelled farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, rising to 83\% and 88\% by 2045. These fractions exceed the corresponding terrain-level fractions because farms occupy the lower, warmer part of each belt, and they are the numbers relevant to growers and lenders rather than to a land-area accounting. The implication extends beyond the case study: wherever cultivable terrain is spatially structured---confined to specific soil types, aspect regimes, parent materials, or slope configurations---climate-only projections will overstate accessible adaptation space \citep{Hijmans2005, Elith2009, Phillips2006, Bunn2015, OvalleRivera2015}. Previous work has noted that climate suitability projections can substantially overestimate available agricultural land where non-climatic constraints such as land use, accessibility, and terrain are not explicitly represented \citep{Bunn2015, OvalleRivera2015}. The degree of overestimation scales with the geographic specificity of the terrain requirement; in orographic specialty crop systems, that specificity is extreme.

The two districts face the same version of this problem, not structurally different ones. \textbf{Kona and Ka\okina u are both spatially constrained.} Their thermal optima are statistically indistinguishable (\S\ref{sec:studyarea}), so a shared warming signal produces nearly identical island-wide thermal trajectories for both districts (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}). What differs is not thermal sensitivity but terrain geometry: each district occupies a compact, elevation-banded footprint with no room to expand, and in both cases warming shifts the thermally-favourable zone toward the upper margin of that fixed footprint, leaving the lower, currently-farmed majority of each belt on a declining trajectory. The risk is structural for both, not exceptional to either: not merely that farms are warming, but that the terrain type those farms occupy limits how much of each belt can track the thermal optimum upslope. The thermal window shifts for both districts; the geomorphological door widens for neither.

For practitioners in both districts, the convergence map translates this shared structural constraint into concrete planning guidance. For Kona and Ka\okina u producers alike, the priority is thermal resilience within the existing topographic footprint: shade management to reduce canopy temperatures, variety trials with higher thermal tolerance, and targeted monitoring at the upper-elevation fringe where the terrain-thermal intersection remains positive through 2035--2045. The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) identifies those fringe cells explicitly for both districts---priority sites for agronomic intervention, not confirmed relocation or expansion targets (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}). Large-scale geographic relocation is constrained by the geometry of each belt; the actionable adaptation space is smaller than unconstrained thermal analysis suggests, for Kona and Ka\okina u alike. Any non-farmed, topographically matched, thermally-gaining cells identified for either district are candidates for expansion feasibility studies only after terrain, soil, and access screening---thermal trajectory second, not first.

The economic stakes follow directly from the terrain geometry, and apply symmetrically precisely because the two districts' thermal niches barely differ (\S\ref{sec:studyarea})---the risk is shared, not district-specific. Roughly 66\% of Kona-appropriate terrain and 68\% of Ka\okina u-appropriate terrain is on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, representing roughly 3,173 of Kona's $\approx$4,800 farmed acres and 810 of Ka\okina u's $\approx$1,200 farmed acres. Because ``100\% Kona'' certification commands premium wholesale prices and the premium is inseparable from geographic identity, thermal resilience in place---shade management, heat-tolerant variety trials---is not a contingency plan but the only viable plan, for both districts. The deeper economic risk is misallocation of adaptation capital driven by a false signal: a planner relying on the unscreened, thermal-only direction-of-change view observes roughly 70\% of farmable island land apparently gaining suitability under either district's envelope, and may rationally treat large-scale relocation or expansion as a near-term option. Restricted to each district's own terrain, roughly two-thirds is instead on a declining trajectory (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}), and a genuine absolute-suitability accounting (\S\ref{sec:methods}) puts terrain-constrained accessible land at 7- to 11-fold less than the thermal-only estimate. Most of that gap is arithmetic---each district's terrain footprint is a small, fixed fraction of the island to begin with (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation})---but the practical implication does not depend on how the gap decomposes: wherever specialty crop premiums are anchored to geographic identity, the price signal will misprice supply risk unless land-use and investment decisions are read against actual terrain, not thermal projections alone.

Several limitations constrain these estimates. \label{sec:discussion:limits} The most consequential is the one quantified in \S\ref{sec:methods}: the temperature field is 98\% explained by elevation, and the topographic screen uses elevation as one of its features, so the thermal and terrain screens are strongly correlated rather than independent. Everything downstream inherits this. In particular, the finding that the gaining fraction of each district's terrain concentrates at the upper elevation margin cannot be cleanly separated from the fact that moving upslope also moves a cell away from its district's elevation centroid; the analysis can show that the thermally-favourable zone migrates upslope while the terrain envelope does not, but it cannot establish that any particular upper-fringe cell constitutes a genuine agro-ecological refuge. A screen built on non-elevation terrain descriptors, or a thermal field with substantially more non-elevation structure than the residual $0.60$\,\textdegree C available here, would be required to resolve that. The observed climatology also does not resolve canopy-level thermal modification by shade trees, which acts at a finer scale than any gridded climate product. Fibre-optic distributed temperature sensing (DTS) along elevation transects would still provide the in-situ calibration data needed to validate the gridded climatology against observed canopy conditions and remains a natural next step. The climatology used here is the Kodama product's full 1990--2021 record (its complete available temporal extent), so it does not share a baseline period with the 2017--2025 window used for the ERA5-Land-derived warming signal $\Delta T$ (\S\ref{sec:data}); reconciling the two periods exactly, rather than combining a long-term temperature normal with a shorter-period warming trend, is left to future revision. Because the topographic screen's own feature set includes mean elevation, the finding that only a narrow upper-elevation fringe of either district's terrain gains thermal suitability under warming (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}) should be read alongside this limitation: the screen cannot, by construction, rule out that this fringe gain is partly an artefact of a cell's own elevation moving it away from its district's centroid, rather than purely a genuine agro-ecological signal, and disentangling the two is left to future work. Precipitation is treated implicitly through its correlation with elevation-derived climate features but is not projected as an independent driver. Hawaiian precipitation projections show larger model spread than temperature, and drought stress may co-constrain expansion terrain in either district in ways the current framework does not capture. The topographic model does not incorporate hydrological access, land tenure, or irrigation infrastructure. Some cells scoring well on the topographic screen may be inaccessible for logistical or legal reasons not captured in the eight-feature topographic screen. None of these limitations alter the structural result that terrain constraints bound the feasible adaptation space; they affect the precise location of that space, not its geometric existence.

The same timescale mismatch operates wherever a crop is terrain-anchored, though the arithmetic of the intersection behaves less simply than a naive terrain-fraction argument implies. Using the formal notation from \S\ref{sec:intro}, when $\mathcal{C}_r \supseteq \mathcal{T}_r$---that is, when virtually the entire terrain-feasible footprint is also thermally suitable---$\mathcal{F}_r = \mathcal{T}_r$, and the overestimation factor reduces to its loosest possible value, $|\mathcal{C}_r|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$, bounded above by $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$, the inverse of the terrain fraction (22.8$\times$ Kona, 20.7$\times$ Ka\okina u). That special case is far from holding here: under the level-set definition (\S\ref{sec:methods}), only 24.2\% of farmable island land exceeds the $\tau=0.5$ threshold under the Kona envelope and 19.2\% under the Ka\okina u envelope (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}), so $\mathcal{C}_r$ is a small subset of $\mathcal{G}$, and $|\mathcal{G}|/|\mathcal{T}_r|$ is a loose bound rather than a tight one. What the realized factors (9.0$\times$ Kona, 7.2$\times$ Ka\okina u) instead reveal is that each district's own terrain is \textit{more} thermally suitable than farmable land generally, not less---because coffee was planted on terrain already close to each district's thermal optimum, the terrain-thermal interaction pulls the realized factor \textit{below} the terrain-fraction bound rather than above it. What survives is a narrower point than the specific bound: a district's terrain footprint concentrates on land disproportionately likely to already be climatically favourable, so an island-wide thermal-only estimate overstates the district's own accessible opportunity regardless of the exact multiplier, because it credits land the district's farms were never sited to occupy in the first place; the geometry recurs wherever a specialty crop is anchored to a geographically restricted terrain configuration. In Colombia, OvalleRivera et al.\ \citep{OvalleRivera2015} identify $\approx$21,900\,km$^2$ of currently climatically suitable Arabica area and project the suitable zone shifting 500--1,300\,m upslope under mid-century warming, creating the appearance of large new opportunity. The authors themselves note that conversion of higher-elevation terrain is uncertain, citing soil and access constraints---precisely the terrain feasibility question our framework makes explicit and quantifiable. As in Kona, the upslope terrain above the current Colombian production band (above $\approx$1,500\,m) transitions to younger, steeper, less-developed substrate that may satisfy a thermal criterion while lacking the agro-ecological structure of the existing farms. Ethiopian highland Arabica, grown on Afromontane terrain with specific geological and moisture dynamics, faces an analogous constraint \citep{Davis2012}. The pattern recurs from Kona to Ka\okina u to Colombia: a terrain configuration fixed on geological or geomorphological timescales does not track a thermal optimum moving on a decadal one. The overestimation is not a Hawaiian peculiarity---it is a structural consequence of evaluating climate suitability over a continuous geographic surface when the crop's realized niche is defined by the intersection of climate and a spatially restricted terrain configuration. The magnitude scales with terrain specificity; the correction is the same regardless of crop or region: apply a feasibility screen before interpreting thermal projections as actionable adaptation opportunity.

% ── 5. Conclusions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusions}

Terrain is fixed on geological timescales; a thermal optimum moves on decadal ones. Where a crop's identity---and its price---is bound to a particular terrain configuration, that mismatch means the optimum can migrate but the crop cannot follow it.

Hawaiian coffee makes the mismatch legible because both sides of it can be measured. Only 3.8\% of the Big Island carries Kona's agro-ecological terrain signature and 4.8\% carries Ka\okina u's, and those footprints are properties of the volcano rather than of the climate. Against them, the thermally suitable area island-wide is essentially static between 2035 and 2045---down 0.4\% under one district's envelope, up 0.7\% under the other's---while the intersection that actually matters contracts by 16\% and 21\%. The loss is produced entirely by the two sets sliding out of register, and it falls hardest on land already in production: 73\% of Kona's and 80\% of Ka\okina u's farm cells are on a declining thermal trajectory by 2035, and 83\% and 88\% by 2045. A climate-only reading of the same warming would report roughly 70\% of farmable island land improving, a figure that is very nearly a restatement of how much of the island lies above 600\,m.

The convergence map (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}) is the decision surface for adaptation planning in both districts---not where coffee might theoretically exist under future temperatures, but where it actually can. Kona and Ka\okina u are both spatially constrained, in the same way and to a similar degree; this is one problem with two local expressions, not two different problems, and relying on thermal projections alone would misdirect both the research investment and the adaptation response for either district.

The argument is transferable wherever a crop's market identity is anchored to a terrain configuration rather than merely to a climate: designation-of-origin viticulture, highland Arabica systems, and any product whose premium survives only on particular ground. In each case the correct question under warming is not how much land becomes climatically suitable, but how long the suitable band and the identity-bearing terrain remain in register---and, when they separate, whether the crop's value can survive the move. That is a question about the intersection of a moving set and a fixed one, and it cannot be answered from a thermal projection alone.

\vspace{10pt}
\hrule
\vspace{10pt}

% ── 6. Methods ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data}
\label{sec:data}

\subsubsection{Geospatial Grid and Farm Boundaries}

Farm boundaries were sourced from the 2020 Hawai\okina i Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021} (674 coffee polygons, $\approx$6{,}000\,acres total), projected to UTM Zone~4N and intersected with a 500\,m grid to yield 471 grid cells labelled by farm-centroid overlap. Of these, 385 had successful climate-variable extraction; after further filtering for complete NDVI and soil coverage, 317 cells (Kona $n = 258$, Ka\okina u $n = 59$) retain every feature and are used throughout the analysis below. Island-wide projections were computed across all valid 500\,m land cells above sea level.

\subsubsection{Terrain Features}

Elevation data from the USGS 3D Elevation Program (3DEP) 1/3 arc-second DEM \citep{USGS3DEP} were mosaic-merged across three tiles and average-resampled to 500\,m. Eight topographic features were computed per cell: mean elevation, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and distance to coastline.

\subsubsection{Vegetation}

Median monthly NDVI composites for 2017--2025 were extracted from Sentinel-2 Surface Reflectance imagery via Google Earth Engine \citep{Gorelick2017}. Four features per cell: annual mean, standard deviation, range, and coefficient of variation.

\subsubsection{Climate}

Baseline climate was sourced from ERA5-Land daily reanalysis \citep{HersbachERA5}, aggregated to 18 annual and seasonal features for 2017--2025. Future projections used delta-method bias correction applied to ACCESS-CM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-HR under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, obtained from the NASA NEX-GDDP-CMIP6 archive \citep{Thrasher2022}, for 20-year windows centred on 2035 and 2045. Ensemble central estimates: $\Delta T_{2035} \approx +1.00$\,\textdegree C, $\Delta T_{2045} \approx +1.35$\,\textdegree C.

\subsubsection{Soil Properties}

Eight SSURGO soil properties \citep{SSURGO} at 0--30\,cm per farm cell: drainage class, depth to restriction, available water capacity, pH, organic matter, sand, silt, and cation exchange capacity.

\subsubsection{Surficial Geology}
\label{sec:data:geology}

Lava-flow age was sourced from the digital geologic database of the Island of Hawai\okina i \citep{Trusdell2006}, which digitises the underlying 1:100{,}000 geologic map of Wolfe and Morris (USGS Geologic Investigations Series I-2524-A, 1996); we used the National Park Service Geologic Resources Inventory shapefile reformatting of that database, comprising 6{,}141 mapped rock-unit polygons joined to their legend entries by unit symbol. Polygons mapped as ``Historic A.D.\ 1790 or younger''---the youngest, least-weathered substrate, including the 1859 and 1950 Mauna Loa flows and the bulk of Kīlauea's recent output---were rasterised onto the same 500\,m UTM grid as the island DEM (\S\ref{sec:methods:farmability}).

\subsection{Topographic Feasibility Screen}
\label{sec:methods:topo}

Eight topographic features (\S\ref{sec:data}) were standardised using a \texttt{StandardScaler} fitted on the 317 labelled farm cells, and regional centroids were computed as the mean standardised feature vector for each district. The feature set---elevation mean, elevation standard deviation, maximum slope, aspect sine, aspect cosine, total relief, local relief, and coastline distance---captures the primary axes of topographic variation that distinguish cultivated terrain on the Big Island: elevation band, slope, aspect orientation, landscape roughness, and coastal proximity. For each island cell, Euclidean distance to each centroid was converted to a continuous similarity score:
\[
    s_r = \frac{1}{1 + d_r}, \quad r \in \{\text{Kona},\; \text{Ka\textquoteleft u}\}
\]
A topographic identity score $\text{identity} = s_{\text{Ka'u}} - s_{\text{Kona}}$ assigns each cell to the closer regional profile: positive values indicate Ka\okina u-like terrain, negative values indicate Kona-like terrain.

Euclidean distance in standardised feature space is appropriate here because the objective is domain bounding rather than probabilistic classification: the screen asks whether a cell falls within the cloud of observed farm terrain, not which class it is most likely to belong to. Standardisation removes scale differences across features; the remaining correlation structure is treated as representing genuinely co-occurring terrain constraints rather than redundancy to be decorrelated. As a robustness check, we repeated the analysis using Mahalanobis distance with a pooled within-class covariance matrix, which fully decorrelates the feature space before computing distances. The Mahalanobis screen produces a smaller Ka\okina u terrain footprint (2.97\% vs.\ 4.85\% at the 95th-percentile baseline), consistent with several features being correlated on island terrain (elevation and coastline distance co-vary; total and local relief overlap). At the 95th-percentile baseline the Mahalanobis screen gives the larger overestimation factor (14.0$\times$ vs.\ 7.2$\times$ Euclidean, level-set definition, \S\ref{sec:methods}), because its smaller footprint excludes a larger share of the thermally-suitable region; the two metrics converge toward each other as the threshold widens (60.7$\times$ Mahalanobis vs.\ 13.7$\times$ Euclidean at the 90th percentile, narrowing to 9.7$\times$ vs.\ 5.5$\times$ at the 97.5th; Supplementary Table~S1). Both metrics agree that terrain-constrained suitability is several-fold smaller than thermal-only suitability at every threshold tested, but the specific factor is sensitive to distance-metric choice and should not be read as a precise, metric-independent number.

This defines a feasibility constraint: terrain unlike existing farms is excluded from suitability scoring regardless of thermal conditions. The screen captures realized agro-ecological structure, not the full fundamental niche, and is intended as a constraint on economically and operationally viable expansion, not biological possibility. The framework is not a predictive classifier of suitability, but a constraint on the geographic domain over which suitability can be meaningfully evaluated. We validate that the screen constrains extrapolation rather than reproducing training locations by evaluating spatial stability under threshold perturbation and contrasting with thermal-only outputs (Figures~\ref{fig:thermal}--\ref{fig:convergence}).

Cells where distance to the nearer centroid exceeds the 95th percentile of within-region farm-to-centroid distances are excluded; this threshold is interpreted as a boundary of observed agro-ecological coherence rather than a statistical cutoff per se. A sensitivity analysis (90th--97.5th percentile range) shows Jaccard similarity to the 95th-pct baseline of 0.92 (Kona) / 0.85 (Ka\okina u) at the 92.5th percentile and 0.86 / 0.76 at the 97.5th, falling to 0.73 / 0.47 at the 90th (Supplementary Fig.~S1). Ka\okina u is the less stable of the two at every threshold, consistent with its centroid being estimated from only 59 farm cells; we read the drop at the 90th percentile as tail instability in a distance distribution estimated from few points, not as an ecological boundary. Critically, the overestimation factor itself---the ratio of thermal-only to terrain-constrained suitable area under the level-set definition (\S\ref{sec:methods})---ranges from 5.5$\times$ (97.5th percentile) to 13.7$\times$ (90th percentile) under Euclidean distance across this sensitivity window (Supplementary Table~S1). That specific magnitude is not threshold-invariant---it is set jointly by the terrain footprint size and by how much of the thermally-suitable area falls inside it---but the terrain footprint is small at every threshold tested (2.3--6.4\% of island land classifying as Ka\okina u-like across the 90th--97.5th percentile range), and a several-fold overestimation follows as a structural consequence, not an artefact of the specific threshold chosen. The screen classifies 3.8\% of island land as Kona-like and 4.8\% as Ka\okina u-like at the 95th-percentile baseline; the remaining 91.3\% shares neither district's agro-ecological signature.

\subsection{Thermal Envelope Model}

Island-wide temperature is estimated from the Kodama et al.\ \citep{Kodama2024} Hawai\okina i climate atlas, a 250\,m gridded monthly temperature product (1990--2021, statewide) built from the Hawai\okina i Climate Data Portal's spatial modelling framework, rather than from a fixed lapse rate or a regression calibrated on the farm cells themselves. We obtained monthly mean-temperature rasters for the Big Island for the product's full 1990--2021 record (384 months) and averaged them into a single climatological-mean raster. A regression fit directly to the 317 farm cells is not used because Kona and Ka\okina u occupy different microclimates on opposite flanks of the volcano, and elevation alone does not predict farm-cell temperature within either district in isolation ($R^2 \approx 0.01$ for a within-district ordinary-least-squares fit against ERA5-Land); a regression pooled across both districts inherits that noise and, when extrapolated island-wide, produces a physically implausible gradient. ERA5-Land's 9\,km reanalysis grid is likewise too coarse to resolve a within-district gradient, so it is not used for the temperature surface either. The Kodama climatology was reprojected from its native geographic (EPSG:4326) grid to the same 500\,m UTM Zone~4N grid as the island DEM (\S\ref{sec:data}) via bilinear resampling, so that the island-wide surface used for $\Delta S_r$ (below) and the temperature sampled at each farm cell's own location are drawn from one consistent grid and scale, rather than mixing a coarse and a fine product. Farm-cell temperatures were sampled from this same 500\,m grid at each cell's UTM centroid. Over Big Island land the climatology ranges 5.6--23.8\,\textdegree C, consistent with the $\approx$22--24\,\textdegree C statewide sea-level mean and the $\approx-6$ to $-6.5$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$ lapse behaviour independently reported for the islands below the $\approx$2{,}150\,m trade-wind inversion \citep{KagawaVivianiGiambelluca2020, GiambellucaNullet1991}, cross-checking the gridded product against the literature values the earlier lapse-rate model was anchored to. Aspect-driven and inversion-layer microclimates not fully captured at 500\,m are discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}.

Separate Gaussian thermal envelopes approximate each district's thermal niche, consistent with unimodal species--environment response assumptions widely used in ecological modelling \citep{Guisan2000}:
\[
    S_r(T,\,\Delta T) = \exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2}
    \left(\frac{T + \Delta T - \mu_r}{\sigma_r}\right)^2\right]
\]
where $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$ are the mean and $1.5\times$ the standard deviation of the Kodama climatology temperature sampled at each farm cell's own location (above) across the 317 farm cells in region $r$ ($\mu_{\text{Kona}}=20.75$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Kona}}=1.27$\,\textdegree C, $n=258$; $\mu_{\text{Ka'u}}=20.87$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma_{\text{Ka'u}}=1.01$\,\textdegree C, $n=59$). Because $\mu_r$ and $\sigma_r$ are computed on the identical surface used to evaluate $S_r$ everywhere else, both are expressed on one consistent temperature scale throughout. The $1.5\times$ inflation reflects within-cell and farm-scale microclimatic variability---shade cover, canopy structure, and very local aspect effects---that is not resolved at 500\,m grid resolution even by a real observed climatology. The inflation factor is not intended to expand the fundamental niche but to approximate unresolved sub-grid variability in microclimate conditions at farm scale; it is a judgement, not a calibrated quantity, and we therefore report its consequences directly. Because $\sigma_r$ does not affect the sign of $\Delta S_r$ under a Gaussian envelope (below), the inflation changes no reported gain/loss cell count or percentage. It does move the level set $\mathcal{C}_r$ and hence the overestimation factor, though modestly: varying the inflation across $1.0\times$, $1.25\times$, $1.5\times$ and $2.0\times$ moves the 2035 factor from 8.1$\times$ to 9.5$\times$ (Kona) and 6.4$\times$ to 7.6$\times$ (Ka\okina u), a smaller spread than either the terrain-threshold or distance-metric choice produces (\S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}). This is a deliberate simplification: using the same thermal model, on the same temperature scale, for both districts and for both the terrain-constrained and unconstrained analyses ensures that the overestimation factor reflects the terrain screen alone, not any residual from model complexity or from mismatched temperature references.

Two properties of this construction constrain how its outputs should be read, and we state both explicitly rather than leave them to be discovered. First, for a Gaussian envelope $\sigma_r$ cancels out of the \textit{sign} of the gain/loss classification regardless of how $T$ is estimated: $\Delta S_r(h) > 0 \iff T < \mu_r - \Delta T_h/2$, independent of $\sigma_r$, for any $T$. $\sigma_r$ therefore affects the \textit{magnitude} of $\Delta S_r$---and hence the level set $\mathcal{C}_r$ and the colour scales in Figures~\ref{fig:thermal}--\ref{fig:convergence}---but never which cells gain or lose. Second, the thermal and terrain screens are not independent. Regressing the Kodama climatology on elevation across Big Island land gives $R^2 = 0.98$ (implied lapse $-5.05$\,\textdegree C\,km$^{-1}$, residual SD $0.60$\,\textdegree C): over this domain the observed temperature field is overwhelmingly a function of elevation, with aspect, cloud-belt and coastal effects entering as a small residual. Because the topographic screen also uses mean elevation as one of its eight features, the two screens share approximately 98\% of their variance in that variable. Using an observed climatology rather than an assumed lapse rate is therefore an improvement in degree---the gain/loss boundary is no longer \textit{exactly} an elevation contour, and the residual carries real aspect and cloud-belt information---but not a resolution in kind: the boundary remains close to a single isotherm and hence close to a single elevation contour (\S\ref{sec:results:thermal}), and the near-identical island-wide gain fractions for the two districts follow directly from their near-identical $\mu_r$. Claims of independence between the two screens would not be supportable, and none are made.

Thermal suitability change at horizon $h$ is:
\[
    \Delta S_r(h) = S_r(T,\,\Delta T_h) - S_r(T,\,0)
\]
Positive $\Delta S$ indicates warming toward greater alignment with the thermal optimum; negative values indicate reduced alignment.

Two distinct quantities use this suitability score in what follows, and we keep them explicitly separate. The \textit{direction-of-change} statistic, $\Delta S_r(h)$ above, classifies each cell as gaining or losing suitability under warming, independent of its absolute suitability level; this is what \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}'s district-specific gain/loss percentages and \S\ref{sec:results:thermal}'s island-wide gain fractions report. The \textit{thermal suitability set} $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$ introduced formally in \S\ref{sec:intro}, by contrast, is a level set: $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T) = \{g \in \mathcal{G} : S_r(T_g, \Delta T) > \tau\}$, the farmable cells whose absolute suitability under future warming exceeds a threshold $\tau$, regardless of whether that represents a gain or a loss relative to baseline. $\mathcal{C}_r(\Delta T)$, not $\Delta S_r(h) > 0$, is what defines the overestimation factor in \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}: a cell can register $\Delta S_r > 0$ while remaining thermally unsuitable in absolute terms (a cold, high-elevation cell warming from a suitability score near 0 to a still-negligible score is technically ``gaining''), and conflating that with genuine thermal suitability would inflate the apparent thermal-only opportunity with land that was never realistically viable. We use $\tau = 0.5$ as the primary threshold and report sensitivity over $\tau = 0.1$--$0.8$ in \S\ref{sec:results:overestimation}.

\subsection{Farmability Screen}
\label{sec:methods:farmability}

All projections are restricted to farmable terrain: maximum slope $\leq 25$\textdegree, excluding cliff margins, \textit{and} not mapped as a lava flow of Historic (A.D.\ 1790 or younger) age (\S\ref{sec:data:geology})---slope alone does not exclude young, barren, unconsolidated lava fields, which can be flat enough to pass a slope-only screen while remaining agriculturally unusable. Of island land, 84.8\% satisfies both criteria: 94.3\% passes the slope criterion alone, and the historic-lava exclusion removes a further 9.5 percentage points (4{,}044 cells; a further 149 historic-lava cells sit on terrain already excluded as too steep, so the two criteria overlap slightly and 9.9\% of island land is historic lava in total). The excluded flows are concentrated on Mauna Loa's southwest rift and Kīlauea's east and southwest rift zones.

\subsection{Terrain-Thermal Convergence Analysis}

The terrain-thermal convergence map is the study's primary analytical product (Figure~\ref{fig:convergence}). For each farmable cell, thermal suitability change is displayed only if the cell passes the topographic feasibility screen, and is colour-coded by the matched regional envelope: Kona-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Kona}}$; Ka\okina u-like cells receive $\Delta S_{\text{Ka'u}}$. Farmable cells outside both profiles are shown in mid-grey---these cells may experience thermally favourable futures but lack the terrain, soil, and microclimate structure of existing coffee country. Steep terrain appears in light grey. The coloured footprint is the only geographic space where thermal adaptation signals are simultaneously grounded in terrain reality.

