% ── Figure Legends ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section*{Figure Legends}

\textbf{Figure 1 $|$} \textbf{Study area.} Coffee farm polygons (orange) from the 2020 Hawai\okina i Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021}. The Kona belt occupies the leeward west flank of Mauna Loa (upper cluster, 592 polygons); Ka\okina u farms sit on the south-facing terrace (lower cluster, 82 polygons). The 674 polygons totalling $\approx$6{,}000\,acres are the full mapped commercial coffee-farm footprint on the Big Island in this dataset---not a claim about the U.S.\ specialty coffee industry as a whole, which includes substantial acreage (e.g.\ Kaua\okina i Coffee, $\approx$3{,}100\,acres) outside it.

\textbf{Figure 2 $|$} \textbf{Topographic identity map.} Each 500\,m island land cell scored by Euclidean distance in standardised 8-feature terrain space to the Kona (blue) and Ka\okina u (red) farm centroids. Cells outside the 95th-percentile centroid-distance threshold for both regions are shown in grey. Only 3.8\% of island land is Kona-like and 4.8\% is Ka\okina u-like---the terrain domain into which all thermal projections must be read. Sensitivity across threshold choices is reported in Supplementary Fig.~S1.

\textbf{Figure 3 $|$} \textbf{Thermal suitability change by $\sim$2035 across the full farmable island surface ($\Delta T \approx +1.00$\,\textdegree C; ensemble mean ACCESS-CM2 $\times$ MPI-ESM1-2-HR, SSP2-4.5 $\times$ SSP5-8.5).} Red = increased thermal alignment; blue = reduced alignment. Grey = slope $>25$\textdegree. \textbf{(a)} Kona thermal envelope ($\mu = 20.75$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma = 1.27$\,\textdegree C). 69.2\% of farmable cells gain thermal suitability by $\sim$2035, easing to 67.9\% by $\sim$2045. \textbf{(b)} Ka\okina u thermal envelope ($\mu = 20.87$\,\textdegree C, $\sigma = 1.01$\,\textdegree C). 70.2\% gain by $\sim$2035, easing to 68.8\% by $\sim$2045. Topographic screen not applied. These maps represent the climate-only, direction-of-change view: for both districts alike, restricting to actual terrain and a genuine absolute suitability threshold reduces accessible thermal opportunity 7- to 11-fold. Compare with Figure~4.

\textbf{Figure 4 $|$} \textbf{Terrain-thermal convergence map---the decision surface of this study.} Top: $\sim$2035 ($\Delta T \approx +1.00$\,\textdegree C); bottom: $\sim$2045 ($\Delta T \approx +1.35$\,\textdegree C). Only cells passing the 95th-percentile topographic screen are coloured, each receiving the thermal delta matched to its regional envelope (Kona-like terrain $\to$ Kona $\Delta S$; Ka\okina u-like terrain $\to$ Ka\okina u $\Delta S$). Mid-grey: farmable terrain outside both profiles. Light grey: non-farmable (slope $>25$\textdegree). The coloured footprint is the constrained feasible set. In both districts, the lower, currently-farmed majority of the belt trends blue (declining); a fringe at the upper elevation margin trends red (gaining), consistent with but not proof of a genuine agro-ecological refuge (\S\ref{sec:discussion:limits}). Climate signals outside the coloured footprint lack terrain grounding and cannot be interpreted as actionable adaptation opportunity for coffee production.

